<article xml:id="week2" label="0002"> <!-- Change label to a random string of numbers for each worksheet. The label shows up in the url of the website and keeps students from searching for other worksheets -->
  <title>MATH 140 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!--START TYPING CONTENT FOR SESSION BELOW HERE-->
  <worksheet>
    <title> Session #2 (Sections 2.1 &amp; 2.2)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you.
      The Math 140 textbook is free, and can be viewed at <url href="https://oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/7d9f5efe-b63b-4c57-8db3-91790deed00d" visual="oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/7d9f5efe-b63b-4c57-8db3-91790deed00d">Texas A&amp;M OAKTrust Digital Repository</url>.
    </p>
    <paragraphs> <!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
      <exercise><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
          Use the graph below to complete the following tasks.
            <image source="h2/images/h2fig1" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of four lines with several points labeled. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of four lines with several points labeled, all on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-8</m> to <m>8</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_1</m> passes through the points <m>(-4,0)</m> and <m>(0,2)</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_2</m> passes through the points <m>(0,5)</m> and <m>(6,5)</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_3</m> passes through the points <m>(-1,0)</m> and <m>(0,-1)</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_4</m> passes through the points <m>(-4,0)</m> and <m>(-4,1)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{A}</m> is at <m>(0,-1)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{B}</m> is at <m>(0,2)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{C}</m> is at <m>(2,-3)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{D}</m> is at <m>(-4,0)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{E}</m> is at <m>(-4,3)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{F}</m> is at <m>(0,5)</m>.
                  The point labeled <m>\textbf{G}</m> is at <m>(6,5)</m>.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Describe the slopes of each of the lines as positive, negative, zero, or undefined.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>l_1</m> has a positive slope, <m>l_2</m> has a zero slope, <m>l_3</m> has a negative slope, <m>l_4</m> has an undefined slope
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              What is the slope of <m>l_1</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}=\dfrac{1}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Classify each of the following statements as "true" or "false".
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The equation of <m>l_4</m> is <m>y=-4</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The equation of <m>l_2</m> is <m>y=5</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The equation of <m>l_1</m>, in point-slope form, is <m>y-5=\dfrac{1}{2}(x-6)</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The equation of <m>l_3</m>, in slope-intercept form, is <m>y+3=-(x-2)</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              False, True, True, False
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise><!-- #2 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Fill in the blanks to make the following statements true.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If a refrigerator is currently priced at its scrap value of <m>\$500</m> in 2023, then the value of the refrigerator in 2070 is ____.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\$500</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If a company's cost equation, in dollars, is <m>C(x)=40x+2100</m>, where <m>x</m> is the number of items produced (and sold), the company's fixed costs are ____ and the cost to produce each item is ____.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\$2100</m>, <m>\$40</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the equation of the line, in slope-intercept form, that passes through the point <m>(-5,8)</m> and has a slope of <m>-2</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y=-2x-2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Determine the slope of the line passing through the points <m>(b,4)</m> and <m>(3,2b)</m> (<m>b</m> should appear in your answer). For what value(s) of <m>b</m> is the slope undefined?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              slope: <m>\dfrac{2b-4}{3-b}</m>, slope is undefined at <m>b=3</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the slope, <m>x</m>-intercept, and <m>y</m>-intercept of the line given by the equation <m>4x-3y=21</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              slope: <m>\dfrac{4}{3}</m>, <m>x</m>-intercept: <m>\left(\dfrac{21}{4},0\right)</m>, <m>y</m>-intercept: <m>\left(0,-7\right)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the equation of the line that intersects the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>x=-6</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>y=\dfrac{5}{9}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y=\dfrac{5}{54}x+\dfrac{5}{9}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
        <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Given the line <m>7x+9y=63</m>, answer the following.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>x</m> increases by <m>2</m> units, what is the corresponding change in <m>y</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y</m> decreases by <m>\dfrac{14}{9}</m> units
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>y</m> increases by <m>3</m> units, what is the corresponding change in <m>x</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>x</m> decreases by <m>\dfrac{27}{7}</m> units
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>x</m> decreases by <m>5</m> units, what is the corresponding change in <m>y</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y</m> increases by <m>\dfrac{35}{9}</m> units
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>y</m> decreases by <m>9</m> units, what is the corresponding change in <m>x</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>x</m> increases by <m>\dfrac{81}{7}</m> units
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Five years after the purchase of a kitchen appliance, its value is <m>\$3700</m>. The appliance reaches its scrap value of <m>\$950</m> thirteen years after purchase. Let <m>t</m> represent the number of years since the appliance was purchased.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For <m>0 \leq t \leq 13</m>, determine the value, <m>V</m> (in dollars), of the appliance as a function of the number of years, <m>t</m>, since its purchase.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>V(t)=-343.75t+5418.75</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For <m>t\gt 13</m>, determine the value, <m>V</m> (in dollars), of the appliance as a function of the number of years, <m>t</m>, since its purchase.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>V(t)=\$950</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Determine and interpret the rate of depreciation.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              The rate of depreciation is <m>-343.75\frac{\$}{\text{year}}</m>, which is the rate of change of our kitchen appliance over time <m>t</m> years.
              The kitchen appliance loses <m>\$343.75</m> of value for each year for the first <m>13</m> years until it reaches its scrap value of <m>\$950</m>.
              After <m>13</m> years, it remains at this value.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Determine and interpret <m>V(15)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>V(15)=\$950</m>, which is the scrap value for the kitchen appliance.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              An item purchased for <m>\$750</m> has a value of <m>\$590</m> after <m>48</m> months. Assuming linear depreciation, how long will it take an item to reach its scrap value for the first time?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
              The scrap value, when not given, is <m>\$0</m>.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>t=225</m> months or <m>18.75</m> years
            </p>
          </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="8"><!-- #8 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A company finds that producing <m>40</m> items costs <m>\$9800</m>, while producing <m>70</m> items costs <m>\$11900</m>. Each item is sold for <m>\$85</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Determine the production cost per item.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\$70</m> per item
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the company's linear profit function.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(x)=15x-7000</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="9"><!-- #9 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              A manufacturer has fixed costs of <m>\$300</m> and a production cost of <m>\$40</m> per item. If the company earns a profit of <m>\$5600</m> by selling <m>200</m> items, what is the company's revenue function? Assume linear cost and profit functions.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>R(x)=69.50x</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="10"><!-- #10 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A company's cost, revenue, and profit functions are graphed below.
            <image source="h2/images/h2fig2" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of three lines with several points labeled. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of three lines with several points labeled, all on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-30</m> to <m>300</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-3000</m> to <m>19000</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_1</m> passes through the points <m>(0,9000)</m> and <m>(150,13500)</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_2</m> passes through the points <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(150,13500)</m>.
                  The line labeled <m>l_3</m> passes through the points <m>(150,0)</m> and <m>(170,1200)</m>.
                  Each point mentioned above is labeled with a solid dot, indicating its position.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Identify which of the three lines (<m>l_1</m>, <m>l_2</m>, and <m>l_3</m>) are the cost function, <m>C(x)</m>, the revenue function, <m>R(x)</m>, and the profit function, <m>P(x)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>l_1=C(x)</m>, <m>l_2=R(x)</m>, <m>l_3=P(x)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the equations that represent each function.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>l_1=30x+9000</m>, <m>l_2=90x</m>, <m>l_3=60x-9000</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              How many items will the company have to sell to reach a monthly profit of <m>\$21720</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>512</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="11"><!-- #11 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Given <m>x</m> represents the number of items supplied or demanded each month, <m>p</m> represents the unit price of the item (in dollars), and the two equations below, answer the following questions.
            <md>
              \text{Equation A: }4p-19x=450\\
              \text{Equation B: }7p+45x=3150
            </md>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Which equation is the demand function? Why?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Equation B is the demand function as it has a negative slope.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              How many items will consumers purchase if the items are free?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>70</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Above what price will consumers not buy the item?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\$450</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Producers will only provide items if the price is above what value?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\$112.50</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="12"><!-- #12 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consumers will buy 200 items at a price of <m>\$420</m> per item. If the price decreases to <m>\$396</m> per item, the consumers will buy <m>600</m> items. Manufacturers will not market (or sell) this item below <m>\$4</m>, but if the price increases by <m>\$3</m> per item, they will be willing to sell <m>25</m> more items. 
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the linear demand function that represents this scenario.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>p(x)=-0.06x+432</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the linear supply function that represents this scenario.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>p(x)=0.12x+4</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 3  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
      
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write the equation of the line that intersects the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>x=-15</m>, and intersects the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>y=3</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=5x+3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=-5x+3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=-\dfrac{1}{5}x-15</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=-\dfrac{1}{5}x+3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=\dfrac{1}{5}x+3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (E)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write the equation of a line that has an undefined slope and passes through the point <m>(4,-3)</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=-3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>x=4</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=4</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>x=-3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=4x-3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>y-2=-\dfrac{2}{3}(x+5)</m>, if the <m>y</m>-value decreases by <m>5</m> units, what is the corresponding change in the <m>x</m>-value?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>x</m> decreases by <m>10.5</m> units
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>x</m> increases by <m>10.5</m> units
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>x</m> increases by <m>7.5</m> units
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>x</m> decreases by <m>7.5</m> units
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  None of these answer choices are correct.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose that a shoe company has figured it costs them <m>\$65</m> to produce a pair of shoes. The company can produce <m>125</m> pairs of shoes for a total cost of <m>\$17300</m>. The company sells each pair of shoes for <m>\$150</m>. Which of the following represents the company's cost function (in dollars) where <m>x</m> is the number of pairs of shoes produced and sold?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>C(x)=65x+17300</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>C(x)=150x</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>C(x)=150x+17300</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>C(x)=65x+9175</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>C(x)=150x+9175</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            A t-shirt company has determined that consumers want <m>7500</m> of their most popular t-shirt when the price for each t-shirt is <m>\$25</m>. When they increase the price to <m>\$35</m>, consumers demand <m>250</m> fewer t-shirts. The company is willing to produce (and sell) <m>500</m> shirts at a price of <m>\$15</m>, but they will not produce (or sell) any shirt for less than <m>\$5</m>. Which of the following represents the company's demand equation (in dollars) when <m>x</m> t-shirts are demanded?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>p(x)=-0.04x+325</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>p(x)=0.02x+5</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>p(x)=-10x+250</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>p(x)=-25x+8125</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>p(x)=50x-250</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (A)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            The value, in dollars, of a machine is <m>V(t)=-1525.5t+15712</m>, where <m>t</m> is the number of years since its purchase. The estimated scrap (or salvage) value of the machine is <m>\$1982.50</m>. How long will it take for the machine to reach its scrap (or salvage) value for the first time?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>10.3</m> years
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>7</m> years
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>9</m> years
